<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>How low should my sodium be for Ménière’s?</h3>
<p>Many clinicians suggest around 1,500–2,000 mg of sodium per day, spread evenly across meals to avoid peaks. The exact target depends on your health history and other medications, so confirm a safe range with your clinician or a registered dietitian.</p>

<h3>Are diuretics safe to take long-term?</h3>
<p>They can be, with the right monitoring. Diuretics may help smooth inner-ear fluid shifts, but they can affect electrolytes, blood pressure, and kidney function. Your clinician may order periodic labs and adjust the dose. Report cramps, dizziness, or unusual fatigue.</p>

<h3>What’s the deal with betahistine?</h3>
<p>Betahistine is used in many countries to reduce vertigo frequency, though research results are mixed. Some people feel better on it. Availability varies by region, and it may not be approved everywhere. Discuss expected benefits, side effects, and alternatives with your clinician.</p>

<h3>Can I fly or exercise with Ménière’s?</h3>
<p>Many people do both. For flying, keep your routine steady: hydration, light meals, and seat support; pack your vertigo kit. For exercise, favor walking, cycling on stable equipment, and strength or balance work. Skip high-risk activities on days you feel off, and consider vestibular physical therapy to build confidence.</p>


